A||||7||Howard_Sacks||Engine Work or Suspension?||||5||
Z||000000||Howard_Sacks||04-16-2001||02:37 PM||||For those of you that read my first post to the forum, I actually managed to get my GT from Rhode Island to South Jersey without a mechanical failure or getting pulled over.  I did get a lot of looks from other drivers on the interstate.  About the insurance issue, RI state police still hasn't finished an accident report.<P>Like my subject says, I'm currently planning to prep my other car for the road this summer.    The problem is I have limited financial resources with unlimited wants.<P>I have a good 2.8v6 with 75k miles in my wrecked 87.  I thought I would take the good v6 and put it into my 86 GT, but upon starting taking out the blown v6 and seeing just how much work an engine swap is I got second thoughts about not going directly to the larger engine (4.9V8 or a 60degree v6)I was going to put in before the accident with my 87.<P>So the question is should I do the major engine work this summer and do suspension when I get around to it or should I keep use the good v6 and do suspension now? <P><BR>Thanks for the help,<P>Howard Sacks<BR>webmaster@saturnet.net<BR>86GT Blown Engine<BR>87GT Hit pretty bad see <A HREF="http://www.saturnet.net/fiero.mov" TARGET=_blank>www.saturnet.net/fiero.mov</A> <BR>(it's been a bad year)||||reg||5
Z||000001||Jay||04-16-2001||03:01 PM||||If you do suspension now it will be done regardless of which engine you have.  If you do an engine swap you'll most likely have to upgrade your suspension anyways.  My vote is to do the suspension and use the good V6.  An engine swap (V8)is something you don't want to rush.  Make sure you have enough time and money to do it correctly and to check everything out and make sure it is safe.<P>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||04-16-2001||11:39 PM||||I second Jay's motion...  I think you will get to drive your car more this summer if you use the 2.8 that you have.  I have a project car that I am putting the 3.4 DOHC in.  Granted I don't have alot of time to work on it but there is a lot to an eng swap.  Although I haven't done my suspension yet, that job seems more straight fwd.  My only advice is to read up (from the archives) on the different forum members who have modified theirs as there is a lot of experience with suspension upgrades here on PFF.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wade87GT||04-17-2001||09:46 AM||||Hey Howard,<P>I tried to view the .mov file about your car and it won't play. I'm using Windows Media Player 7 and RealPlayer8. Any ideas?<P>Wade||||reg||5
Z||000004||Old Lar||04-17-2001||10:24 AM||||Limited funds dictate that you put your good 2.8 engine, replacing the blown 2.8, until such time you have the resources to put the bigger engine in.  This engine swap which is the easiest of swaps (if there is such a thing), will give you the idea of how much fun you'll have for bigger projects.  There will be limited wiring hassles too.||||reg||3
Z||000005||Howard_Sacks||04-17-2001||04:31 PM||||Ok, thanks for the advice, I thought (was hoping) someone would say something like do the bigger engine first becuase you don't know what kind of spring rates your going to need until you get the engine in.  That way I could justify the big engine.<P>I should probably start another thread for this but, how dumb would it be for me to put a hotter cam in the good 2.8 while it's out of the car?<P>wade: you need quicktime (www.apple.com/quicktime/)<p>[This message has been edited by Howard_Sacks (edited 04-17-2001).]||||reg||1
